<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Media (region)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Media was an ancient region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagros-mountains">Zagros</a> mountains and the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamadan">Hamadan</a>. Home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>, it was the core of the Median kingdom of the seventh and sixth centuries BCE and then a rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrapy</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> it was divided, the north becoming the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/media-atropatene">Media Atropatene</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/media-region.jpg" alt="Media (region)" width="320" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adrien-Hubert Brué · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brue,_Adrien_Hubert,_Asie-Mineure,_Armenie,_Syrie,_Mesopotamie,_Caucase._1839._(CG).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 70s CE, according to Josephus, Alans crossed the Caucasus passes and raided <b>Media</b> and Armenia, nearly capturing the Armenian king Tiridates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The greater danger came from the north: the Alans, and later the Huns and other steppe peoples, crossed the Caucasus by the mountain passes and by the narrow Caspian coastal route at Derbent, and in 135 an Alan invasion struck Albania and <b>Media</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Sargon II claimed the conquest, crushed a further revolt in 720 BCE, deported some 27,000 inhabitants to Mesopotamia and <b>Media</b>, settled people from other lands in their place, and made Samaria the centre of the Assyrian province of Samerina.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The war between Lydia and <b>Media</b> reportedly ended when a solar eclipse darkened a battle in 585 BCE, which Greek tradition said Thales of Miletus had predicted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Media</b> remained a leading Near Eastern power until its absorption by Cyrus the Great around 550 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The transformation from a small regional kingdom into a major imperial power came under Mithridates I (r. c. 171–132 BCE), who conquered <b>Media</b> and, around 141 BCE, the Seleucid heartland of Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
